.TH GRAPHS.CFG 5 "Version 4.3.30:  4 Sep 2019" "Xymon"
.SH NAME
graphs.cfg \- Configuration of the showgraph CGI

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B $XYMONHOME/etc/graphs.cfg

.SH DESCRIPTION
.I showgraph.cgi(1)
uses the configuration file $XYMONHOME/etc/graphs.cfg to
build graphs from the RRD files collected by Xymon.

.SH VOCABULARY
Four terms are used consistently in this page and in the keywords below:
.IP "metric"
one measured quantity (one DS: line of an RRD) - the curves drawn on an image.
.IP "instance"
one measured object (a filesystem, a disk, an interface) - one RRD file.
Instances are what filters match, counts count, and paging slices.
.IP "graph"
one [section] definition in this file.
.IP "image"
one rendered picture (a paging slice). MAXINSTANCESPERIMAGE instances fill one image.
.PP
Historical names are kept as frozen aliases and documented against these
terms: the third field of a GRAPHS environment entry ("name:partname:N",
internally "maxgraphs") is the historical spelling of MAXINSTANCESPERIMAGE; the
"<!-- linecount=N -->" status hint is the historical spelling of an
instance count.

.SH FILE FORMAT
Each definition of a graph type begins with a "[SERVICE]" indicator,
this is the name passed as the "service" parameter to
.I showgraph.cgi(1).
If the service name passed to showgraph.cgi is not found, it will
attempt to match the service name to a graph via the TEST2RRD environment
variable. So calling showgraph.cgi with "service=cpu" or "service=la"
will end up producing the same graph.

A graph definition needs to have a TITLE and a YAXIS setting.
(The X-axis is always time-based).

A definition may also carry "MAXINSTANCESPERIMAGE N": how many of the matched RRD
files are drawn per image when a status or trends page splits this
graph. This keeps the split size with the graph it describes; it
overrides the legacy "name::N" suffix in the GRAPHS environment
variable (see xymonserver.cfg(5)), which remains supported.

"EXSTOREPATTERN <regex>" and "STOREPATTERN <regex>" filter which
instances are stored at all, for every writer path targeting this
graph's files: instances matching EXSTOREPATTERN are never written,
and when STOREPATTERN is set only matching instances are. Patterns
are matched (unanchored, so a substring hit counts - anchor with "^"
and "$" for a whole-name match) against the RRD filename minus its
".rrd" suffix, case-insensitively. Note this differs from FNPATTERN/
EXFNPATTERN, which match the full name including ".rrd". A graph with
storage filters has a file set that diverges from what a status
message shows, so a message-derived paging count cannot be trusted:
the count comes from the fileset index instead, with an unsliced
render only when no index is reachable. (These keywords replace the drafted RRDEXCLUDE/RRDINCLUDE
environment variables; analysis.cfg's "DISK ... IGNORE" remains the
tool when the status line should disappear too.)
.sp
Writer-side keywords (EXSTOREPATTERN, STOREPATTERN) are read
once at xymond_rrd startup, so a change to them takes effect only
after xymond_rrd is restarted; the presentation keywords are re-read
by the CGIs on every page request.
.sp
An "EXSTALEPATTERN <regex>" keyword exempts matching instances from
staleness. The freshness window itself is one fixed number - 86400
seconds (one day), main's historic value: RRD files older than that
are hidden from graphs requested with showgraph.cgi's "nostale"
option - the graph links Xymon's own pages generate always pass it; a
hand-written URL without it shows every file - and the writer-kept
fileset index applies the same window to its paging counts, so the
count always equals what renders. Instances the pattern matches are
NEVER considered stale: they always render and always count. Use it
for legitimately periodic instances - a weekly backup mount, a
monthly job - whose graph must stay visible between appearances
(e.g. "EXSTALEPATTERN backup"). The pattern is matched like the
storage patterns (against the filename minus its ".rrd" suffix,
case-insensitively, unanchored). An exempted instance that is truly
gone stays visible until its RRD file is deleted - the file is easy
to spot, its mtime stops moving. Note the window has a practical
floor: freshness advances only as fast as the update cache writes
(up to ~1h behind), which the fixed one-day window absorbs.
.sp
A "GRAPHOPTIONS <options>" keyword passes extra rrdtool graph options
for this graph. When set it REPLACES the global RRDGRAPHOPTS
environment (see xymonserver.cfg(5)) for this graph - the two are not
combined. Static Y-range options placed here (-u/-l,
--upper-limit/--lower-limit) are dropped whenever the viewer supplies
a range - the zoom feature - so a zoom box always wins; prefer
declaring fixed ranges only where zooming that graph is pointless.
.sp
A "THRESHOLDS OFF" keyword suppresses the automatic co-plot of
threshold relations a self-describing METRICS block declared (see
xymond_rrd(8)): the threshold curves and HRULEs disappear from the
synthesized graph, while the threshold datasets still never plot as
peer metrics. The default (no keyword, or "THRESHOLDS ON") co-plots
on single-instance images. A section may carry only this keyword -
the graph definition itself stays synthesized; a hand-written
definition always has the last word.

A "TRENDS" keyword makes the graph a member of the trends-column page
without an entry in the GRAPHS environment variable - the membership
lives with the graph definition. Graphs listed in GRAPHS remain
members as before.

"INCLUDE <name>" inherits an earlier-defined graph: its header
keywords (TITLE, YAXIS, FNPATTERN, MAXINSTANCESPERIMAGE, TRENDS, ...) are copied,
and its definition lines are copied in place. The variant's own
keywords override the inherited ones, wherever they appear relative
to the INCLUDE line, and its definition lines append - so a variant can retitle, retune the split
size, or add curves without repeating the base. Two caveats: the base
must be defined earlier in the file (a forward reference inherits
nothing), and the keyword must be written in upper case - a lowercase
"include" is the configuration reader's file-inclusion directive
(include <filename>), processed before graphs are parsed:
.sp
[diskio_ops_wide]
.br
        INCLUDE diskio_ops
.br
        TITLE Disk operations (wide)
.br
        MAXINSTANCESPERIMAGE 5

.IP "\fBTITLE\fR"
text shown as the title of the graph.
.IP "\fBYAXIS\fR"
text shown as the YAXIS heading.
.PP
If a fixed set of RRD files are used for the graph, you just write those
in the RRDtool definitions. Note that Xymon keeps all RRD files for a host
in a separate directory per host, so you need not worry about the hostname
being part of the RRD filename.

For graphs that use multiple RRD files as input:
.IP "\fBFNPATTERN\fR"
a filename pattern.
.IP "\fBEXFNPATTERN\fR"
optionally, a pattern of files
to exclude from the graph (see "[tcp]" for an example).
.PP
When FNPATTERN is used, you can use
.IP \(dq@RRDFN@\(dq
in the RRDtool definitions
to pick up each filename.
.IP \(dq@RRDIDX@\(dq
is an index (starting at 0) for each
file in the set.
.IP \(dq@RRDPARAM@\(dq
contains the first word extracted from the
pattern of files (see e.g. "[memory]" how this is used).
.IP \(dq@COLOR@\(dq
picks
a new color for each graph automatically.
Aggregate values across all files selected by FNPATTERN can be built with
"@AVG:name@", "@SUM:name@", "@MIN:name@", "@MAX:name@", "@AVGNAN:name@",
"@SUMNAN:name@", "@MINNAN:name@", "@MAXNAN:name@", "@COUNT:name@",
"@MEDIAN:name@", "@STDEV:name@", "@PERCENT:name:pct@" and "@Ppct:name@"
(short form, e.g. "@P95:name@" or "@P99.9:name@"). The "name" portion
is the variable-name prefix used by preceding "DEF:name@RRDIDX@=..."
lines -- the aggregate expands to "name0,name1,...,nameN-1" followed
by the operator's RPN. "pct" is a decimal percentile (0-100, at most
one dot).
For example, after defining "DEF:t@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:ds0:AVERAGE", the
line "CDEF:avg=@AVG:t@" expands to "CDEF:avg=t0,t1,+,t2,+,3,/" when
three files are matched; "@P95:t@" expands to the matching RRDtool
PERCENT RPN; "@PERCENT:t:99.9@" is the long form of "@P99.9:t@".

When the surrounding graph is sliced by "first"/"count" CGI parameters,
the aggregates honour the slice: only the selected RRDs (firstidx
through lastidx inclusive) contribute. An empty selection yields
"UNKN" for arithmetic ops and "0" for "@COUNT:" (so dividing a
"@SUMNAN:" by a "@COUNT:" stays safe on the empty set: 0/0 in RRDtool
RPN is UNKN as well).

Semantics that rely on RRDtool's RPN operators (so they match what
"rrdgraph_rpn(1)" describes): "@STDEV:" emits the *population* standard
deviation (divides by N, not N-1). "@MEDIAN:" returns the lower middle
value when N is even. "@AVG:" divides by the selected count, not the
non-NaN count -- if any contributor is UNKN the result is UNKN; for a
NaN-aware mean combine "@SUMNAN:" with "@COUNT:".

Names accept alphanumerics and underscore (and the inner ":" in the
"@PERCENT:name:pct@" form). "@DSIDX@"/"@PREVDSIDX@"-style index tokens
must NOT appear inside an aggregate operand -- they are not expanded
recursively, and the aggregate parser stops at the first "@" when
locating the operand terminator.

For graphs that repeat a similar line per data source within a single
RRD file (e.g. ping1..pingN slots of a SmokePing-style RRD), use the
"DSCOUNT N" directive together with the "@DSIDX@" token. At parse
time, every def line containing "@DSIDX@" is expanded into N copies
with @DSIDX@ replaced by 1..N. The companion "@PREVDSIDX@" token
expands to (current index \- 1); a line that uses it automatically
loops 2..N to avoid producing an invalid "ping0" reference. To
override the loop's lower bound for a specific line, prefix it with
"@DSSTART:K@" \- the prefix is stripped and K becomes the new lower
bound. If DSCOUNT is omitted but @DSIDX@ is used, the count is read
at render time from the actual RRD file, so a block doesn't need to
repeat the count and hosts with different sample counts each get
every band.

.PP
For a bundle viewed one service at a time (e.g. "tcp:conn"), FNPATTERN
capture group 1 must be exactly the service component: showgraph uses it
both as the legend (@RRDPARAM@) and to select the single service \- see
"[tcp]", where "^tcp.(.+).rrd" captures "conn" from tcp.conn.rrd.

To draw the total of a dataset across all matched files, define the
per-file DEF and sum it with "@SUMNAN:" (unknown values count as 0;
use "@SUM:" if one silent contributor should make the total unknown
instead). Such a graph should render unsliced \- a paged aggregate
would show partial sums \- so either give the entry no split size or
have the status declare it with instances=all (see xymond_rrd(8) on
self-describing statuses). Example \- one curve with the total read
rate across all disks:
.sp
[diskio_ops_total]
.br
        FNPATTERN ^diskio_ops\\.(.+)\\.rrd
.br
        TITLE Total disk operations
.br
        YAXIS Operations/sec
.br
        DEF:t@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:reads:AVERAGE
.br
        CDEF:rtotal=@SUMNAN:t@
.br
        LINE2:rtotal#00CC00:total reads
.sp
Alternatively "@STACKIT@" stacks the per-file curves, whose silhouette
is also the sum while keeping each file's contribution visible.

The remainder of the lines in each definition are passed directly to the
RRDtool rrd_graph() routine.

The following is an example of how the "la" (cpu) graph is defined. This
is a simple definition that uses a single RRD-file, la.rrd:
.sp
.nf
[la]
\&        TITLE CPU Load
\&        YAXIS Load
\&        DEF:avg=la.rrd:la:AVERAGE
\&        CDEF:la=avg,100,/
\&        AREA:la#00CC00:CPU Load Average
\&        \-u 1.0
\&        GPRINT:la:LAST: \e: %5.1lf (cur)
\&        GPRINT:la:MAX: \e: %5.1lf (max)
\&        GPRINT:la:MIN: \e: %5.1lf (min)
\&        GPRINT:la:AVERAGE: \e: %5.1lf (avg)\en
.fi
.sp

Here is an example of a graph that uses multiple RRD-files, determined
automatically at run-time via the FNPATTERN setting. Note how it uses
the @RRDIDX@ to define a unique RRD parameter per input-file, and the
@COLOR@ and @RRDPARAM@ items to pick unique colors and a matching text
for the graph legend:
.sp
.nf
[disk]
\&        FNPATTERN ^disk(.*).rrd
\&        TITLE Disk Utilization
\&        YAXIS % Full
\&        DEF:p@RRDIDX@=@RRDFN@:pct:AVERAGE
\&        LINE2:p@RRDIDX@#@COLOR@:@RRDPARAM@
\&        \-u 100
\&        \-l 0
\&        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:LAST: \e: %5.1lf (cur)
\&        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:MAX: \e: %5.1lf (max)
\&        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:MIN: \e: %5.1lf (min)
\&        GPRINT:p@RRDIDX@:AVERAGE: \e: %5.1lf (avg)\en
.fi

.SH "ADVANCED GRAPH TITLES"
Normally the title of a graph is a static text defined in
the graphs.cfg file. However, there may be situations where
you want to use different titles for the same type of graph, e.g.
if you are incorporating RRD files from MRTG into Xymon. In that
case you can setup the TITLE definition so that it runs a custom
script to determine the graph title. Like this:
.sp
.nf
\&	TITLE exec:/usr/local/bin/graphitle
.fi
.sp
The \fB/usr/local/bin/graphtitle\fR command is then called with
the hostname, the graphtype, the period string, and all of the
RRD files used as parameters. The script must generate one line of output,
which is then used as the title of the graph. Each of the RRD pathname
parameters will be enclosed in double quotes.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBTEST2RRD\fR"
Maps service names to graph definitions.

.SH NOTES
Most of the RRD graph definitions shipped with Xymon have been ported
from the definitions in the \fBlarrd\-grapher.cgi\fR CGI from LARRD 0.43c.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonserver.cfg(5), rrdtool(1), rrdgraph(1)
